WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.004 --> 00:00:14.006
GHOST SHORT · SOLUTION
Cut the sliver, then fit the fuse
Diagnosis (see "How to approach"): a hairline copper sliver shorts the fused +5 V trace to the ground trace beside it, F1 is blown, R2 is missing.
Order matters: remove the short, prove it is gone, then fit a new fuse.
Tools: Trace cutter (7) · Multimeter (2) · Soldering iron (3) · Select (1)

2
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:22.948
STEP 1 · LOCATE
A hairline of copper runs from the +5 V trace to the ground trace beside it.

3
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:39.432
STEP 2 · REMOVE THE SHORT
Trace cutter (7): cut through the sliver.
Why: On a real board you would scrape it away with a scalpel and check under magnification that no copper bridges the gap.

4
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:51.341
STEP 3 · PROVE IT
Power off, F1's output to ground again: OL. The short is gone.
Why: Never power up on hope. With the short proven gone, a new fuse will survive.

5
00:00:51.342 --> 00:01:00.322
STEP 4 · NEW FUSE
Soldering iron (3) on F1: fit a fresh 0.5 A fuse.

6
00:01:00.323 --> 00:01:12.649
STEP 5 · FIT R2
Select R2's empty footprint and fit 10 kΩ.
Why: Equal resistors split the voltage in half: 5 V × 10k / (10k + 10k) = 2.5 V.

7
00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:17.744
STEP 6 · CHECK
Power on: TP1 reads +2.499 V, and the power LED D1 is lit.

8
00:01:17.745 --> 00:01:33.186
STEP 7 · VERIFY
Run the test bench (Run tests).
Why: It powers the board, checks every objective, and only decrypts the flag if the circuit now matches the original design: a fix that merely works around a fault does not count.

9
00:01:33.187 --> 00:01:36.788
STEP 7 · VERIFY
All objectives pass and the flag is captured. Earn your own: it is blurred here.

10
00:01:36.789 --> 00:01:48.091
WHAT YOU LEARNED
Order of a safe repair
Find the short → remove it → prove it is gone with Ω → only then replace the fuse.
A divider of two equal resistors gives exactly half the input.
